<rdf:Description rdf:about='http://ecoinformatics.org/oboe/oboe.1.0/oboe-standards.owl#Siemens'>
 <rdf:type rdf:resource='http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept'/>
 <skos:altLabel xml:lang='en'>S</skos:altLabel>
 <skos:broader rdf:resource='http://ecoinformatics.org/oboe/oboe.1.0/oboe-core.owl#DerivedUnit'/>
 <skos:definition xml:lang='en'>The siemens (symbol: S) is the SI derived unit of electric conductance and electric admittance. Conductance and admittance are the reciprocals of resistance and impedance respectively, hence one siemens is equal to the reciprocal of one ohm, and is sometimes referred to as the mho. It is named after the German inventor and industrialist Ernst Werner von Siemens. In English, the term siemens is used both for the singular and plural. The 14th General Conference on Weights and Measures approved the addition of the siemens as an SI derived unit in 1971. It is a composite unit in OBOE because it is composed of 2 base units, and OBOE-derived units are a single base unit raised to a power. A siemens is Ampere/volt</skos:definition>
 <skos:inScheme rdf:resource='http://opendata.inra.fr/anaeeThes/'/>
 <skos:prefLabel xml:lang='en'>siemens</skos:prefLabel>
 <skos:scopeNote rdf:datatype='http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string'>This concept has been imported from the AnaEE ontology Unit Branch classes</skos:scopeNote>
</rdf:Description>

